On Wed, 27 Jul 2005, Jove wrote:

> On 25 Jul 2005 04:14:38 -0700, dogscoff@eudoramail.com wrote:
>
>   The nethack scoring system works just fine at the low to mid
> player skill levels.  It gives players with no realistic hope
> of ascending something to shoot for.
>
>   IMHO this is why #quitting can give you a slightly higher score
> than waiting one more turn to die and why #quitters are recorded in
> the logfile.  It's to encourage lower level players to learn to
> recognize when they're about to die.  Probably the most important
> skill in Nethack.


>           - Rank any game ended by quitting ahead of any game ended
>             by player death, and below any game ended by escaping.

I don't agree with that since that would encourage people in though
situation (eg hallucinating and with a cursed ring of polymorph) to #quit
instead of looking for an intelligent solution to their problem, which is
not the best way to improve playing skill.
Plus #quitting when you're at 1HP surrounded by Liches and Dragon in no
way is a good thing. It's *before* that you should have done something
about it and I don't see why somebody trapped in such a situation would
have more reward that someone who try to avoid the same fight at first.

I'm rather in the "no #quitter in the high score" mood. (and there's a
patch for it).

The point is that the important stuff is not to recognize when you're
about to die but to recognize the situation where fighting means death and
running away means life. That has nothing to do with encouraging
#quitters.


For the rest, I kinda agree. Just notice that "You genocide 2 species" or
"You wish for 5 objects" are considered conduct in the endgame statistics
iirc and so the "ascension with conduct" category should be a bit more
restricted.

